Why This Is Asked

Tests DP for non-overlapping intervals with fixed cost and gaps, reflecting scheduling problems.

Key Concepts

- DP with dp[i][t] for first i days and t windows
- Prefix sums for quick window sums
- End-at-i transitions with a preceding gap
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Time: O(n^2 k); Space: O(nk)

Code Example

```javascript
function maxProfitKWindows(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prv = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) {
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) {
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      if (t>0) {
        for (let s=1;s<=i;s++) {
          if (i- s >= 2) {
            const val = dp[s-2][t-1] + (S[i]-S[s-1]) - c;
            if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; prv[i][t] = s; }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {best: dp[n][k], endIndex: n, t: k};
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(nk) using monotone queues? 
- How would you reconstruct the exact windows from the DP table?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model constraints with DP, including exact count, non-adjacency, and reconstruction; this is a common interview pattern for medium-difficulty tasks.

## Key Concepts

- 2D DP with adjacency constraint
- Exact-count optimization
- Backtracking to recover indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPromo(revenue, k, cost){
  const n = revenue.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], dp[i-1][t]);
      // take i
      if (t > 0){
        const takeFrom = i >= 2 ? dp[i-2][t-1] : (t-1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG);
        const val = takeFrom + revenue[i-1] - cost;
        dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i][t], val);
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction
  let i = n, t = k;
  const picks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t >= 0){
    if (dp[i][t] === dp[i-1][t]){ i--; }
    else { picks.unshift(i); i -= 2; t--; }
  }
  return { best: dp[n][k], picks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize space to O(k)?
- How to handle ties in reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests partition DP with a subarray range cost, requiring precomputation of subarray extrema and careful reconstruction. It adds a different objective than prior questions by using (max-min) rather than a function of endpoints or a fixed multiplier. 

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions with exact-k segments
- Subarray range queries (max-min) via precomputation
- Path reconstruction from parent pointers
- Time/space trade-offs for small to mid-size n

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const maxv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  const minv = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    maxv[i][i] = A[i];
    minv[i][i] = A[i];
    for (let j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
      maxv[i][j] = Math.max(maxv[i][j-1], A[j]);
      minv[i][j] = Math.min(minv[i][j-1], A[j]);
    }
  }
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) dp[i][1] = (maxv[0][i-1]-minv[0][i-1]) * i, par[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p <= i-1; p++) {
        const cost = (maxv[p][i-1]-minv[p][i-1]) * (i-p);
        if (dp[p][t-1] + cost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[p][t-1] + cost;
          par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let idx = n, rem = k, cuts = [];
  while (rem > 0) {
    const p = par[idx][rem];
    cuts.push([p, idx-1]);
    idx = p; rem--;
  }
  cuts.reverse();
  return {cost: dp[n][k], blocks: cuts};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: fixed window size with non-overlap, maximizing sum of window maxima.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute windowMax to make transitions O(1).
- Prefix DP with non-overlapping constraint.
- Reconstruction via a take/parent table.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKWindows(A, n, k, m){
  const windowMax = Array(n+1).fill(-Infinity);
  for(let i=m; i<=n; i++){ // window ending at i
    let mx = -Infinity;
    for(let j=i-m+1; j<=i; j++) mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
    windowMax[i] = mx;
  }
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0; i<=n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let t=1; t<=k; t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if(i>=m && dp[i-m][t-1] + windowMax[i] > dp[i][t]){
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-m][t-1] + windowMax[i];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests partitioning a sequence into exactly k blocks where block value is non-linear in length. It requires a careful DP with reconstruction and shows understanding of prefix sums and state transitions.

## Key Concepts

- DP over partitions
- Prefix sums for O(1) subarray sums
- Non-linear block value and reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space

## Code Example

